  <p>Here is what keeps Mitchell's SDET candidates awake at night — and what should keep you awake: <strong>in 2026, snapshot testing is no longer a Jest novelty. Visual regression platforms like Percy, Chromatic, and BackstopJS have made snapshot testing a core part of the frontend quality pipeline. Every design-system team uses snapshot tests to catch unintended visual changes. Every hiring manager at a company that builds user-facing software expects you to discuss snapshot testing intelligently — not as a "nice to have," but as a strategic decision about when serialised output is the right verification mechanism and when it is a lazy substitute for thoughtful assertions. Can you explain why a snapshot of a 3,000-line React component tree is an anti-pattern — and what you would replace it with? Can you articulate the difference between snapshot testing for regression detection and snapshot testing for behavioural specification — and why confusing the two leads to tests that are neither good regressions nor good specifications? Can you have the conversation with a frontend developer who says "just update the snapshots — they always break when I change the button colour" — and explain why that reflex is training the team to ignore snapshot diffs, which means the one real regression in a sea of false positives will also be ignored?</strong></p>
