        a: "Snapshot testing captures the serialised output of a function or component and saves it to a file. In Jest, you use <code>toMatchSnapshot()</code> or <code>toMatchInlineSnapshot()</code> in your test. On the first run, Jest creates a snapshot file in a <code>__snapshots__</code> directory. On subsequent runs, Jest compares the current output against the saved snapshot. If they differ, the test fails, and you must either fix the regression (if the change is unintended) or update the snapshot (if the change is intentional) using <code>jest --updateSnapshot</code> or the interactive <code>u</code> key in watch mode. Snapshot testing is a regression-detection mechanism — it tells you that something changed, not whether the change is correct. It is most valuable for testing structured output with many fields (API responses, component render trees, configuration objects) where writing individual assertions for every field is verbose and fragile. It is least valuable for testing specific behaviours where a targeted assertion — <code>expect(button.text).toBe('Submit')</code> — is clearer and more maintainable than a 400-line component-tree snapshot."
